The block develops conjugate geometry with the physics-style convention explicit and translates Axler's opposite slot convention. It proves complex projection/Cauchy–Schwarz, adjoint identities, real Hermitian eigenvalues, unitary norm preservation, a complete decomposition for an explicit Hermitian involution and the metric carried by an oblique basis change. General finite spectral theory is not silently assumed. The original family H=((d,z),(conj(z),−d)), d²+|z|²=1, has explicit orthogonal projectors; its d=3/5,z=4i/5 case connects eigencomponents, changes of basis and coupled evolution.

Complex and matrix exponentials are defined through absolutely controlled series. Uniform derivative bounds justify the evolution equation, the same-generator product law and initial-value uniqueness. A submultiplicative row-sum bound gives the explicit geometric-tail certificate for finite truncation. The H²=I example yields cos(t)I−i sin(t)H and conserved norm/quadratic form. Noncommuting transformations change component results despite norm preservation; Euler stepping spuriously multiplies squared norm by 1+h² per step. The text separates these mathematical statements from a Hamiltonian, Born probabilities or any empirical quantum claim.

The new `math-source-operators` Record identifies Sheldon Axler's fourth edition, 2024, English PDF revision **30 October 2026**, CC BY-NC 4.0 with component exceptions. Actual selected inner-product, adjoint, self-adjoint and unitary passages were read, and example 7.2 supplies a bounded rectangular-adjoint reconstruction. The existing `th-ode` Record gained only an inspection note: MIT's four-page Matrix Exponentials note was read and its page-3 matrices visually checked. Examples 3B/3C supply nilpotent/coupled initial-value reconstructions; the common text adds the uniform argument where the short note assumes termwise differentiation. Broader book/course sections remain assignments. Source media are external, and no author code was copied. The previously suggested Hefferon identity was corrected during inspection: the existing engineering linear-algebra source is Boyd/Vandenberghe, retained unchanged.

